{https://youtu.be/C3h3t6GxTH8?si=1sbvda_e5XMaXvUa&t=121} 1. e4 {[%clk 0:04:59] } e6 {[%clk 0:04:59] } 2. d4 {[%clk 0:04:57] } d5 {[%clk 0:04:49] } 3. exd5 {[%clk 0:04:53] this is the exchange but I'm keeping it because it could sort of transpose from a Stonewall attempt when white plays the early Bg5} exd5 {[%clk 0:04:39] } 4. Nf3 {[%clk 0:04:52] } Bd6 {[%clk 0:04:21] } (4... f5 5. c4 Nf6 6. Bd3 Bb4+ 7. Nc3 O-O 8. cxd5 Re8+ 9. Be2 Nxd5 10. O-O {if we try to force the SW here we may end up in this kind of uncomfortable position (for a wonestall enjoyer)}) 5. Bg5 {[%clk 0:04:45] If we'd have tried to have gone into the stonewall with an early Bd6 for some reason then the follow up from white Bg5 may prompt us to switch to the French set up with f6, Ne2, Nc6 etc., set up.} f6 {[%clk 0:04:11] Repel the bishop. Aman plans a set up from here knights on e7, c6, LSB on f5 or g4 and potentially long castle after Qd7.} 6. Be3 {[%clk 0:04:35] } Nc6 {[%clk 0:04:07] } 7. Bb5 {[%clk 0:04:32] } Ne7 {[%clk 0:04:05] we are now happy to play a6 next move because Bxc6, Nxc6 would basically win us the bishop pair for nothing.} 8. Nc3 {[%clk 0:04:26] A bit of a tricky move in that now Bxc6, Nxc6 would leave the d pawn hanging to Nxd5.} Be6 {[%clk 0:03:49] We had plans for Bf5 or Bg4 but we play Be6 to defend the pawn. This wasn't 100% necessary as the bxc6 recapture was possible and we'd still be OK with that position but this is slightly better in the sense it keeps the pawn structure intact.} 9. Bxc6+ {[%clk 0:04:05] } Nxc6 {[%clk 0:03:48] Positionally we are already better. We control g5 and e5 with our f pawn and prevent knight infiltration. Same for e4 c4 controlled by our d pawn. We have the bishop pair, and Aman thinks that the light squares will be important this game. White's d pawn is fixed on a dark square.} 10. Qd2 {[%clk 0:03:50] Smart move by the opponent. Since he is facing the bishop pair a smart plan would be to trade one of the bishops off. (Silman also talks about the importance of this in Amateurs Mind). So his next move could be Bf4 offering the trade of DSBs.} Qd7 {[%clk 0:03:22] On Bf4 we will not be interested in trading, and would likely play Be7 or Bb4.} 11. O-O-O {[%clk 0:03:40] maybe our opponent played Qd2 with the idea of O-O-O and was not thinking about the need to trade off one of our bishop pair.} O-O-O {[%clk 0:03:07] Aman felt either O-O or O-O-O were both fine here. g5 a potential follow up idea to stop Bf4.} 12. Nb5 {[%clk 0:03:35] Seems he's after our bishop this way. Aman doesn't mind the trade as we'd still have the LSB/opposite color bishop and could launch some decent attacks.} g5 {[%clk 0:02:19] Allowing the N for B trade. This stops Bf4. Also ideas to bring the rook across to the e file. Aman notes here that while he is file with N for B what he wants to avoid is DSB for DSB which would leave Aman with a LSB vs N potentially which Aman worries might be weak.
Aman also notes here that when you have opposite colored bishops it makes sense to place your pawns on the same color complex as your opponent's bishop. So moves like b6, Kb7, h6 would be ideas as follow ups.
This is interesting because as we go into an end game with opposite colored bishops (or, anyway, with N vs B where we have the N) Aman often does the opposite, putting his pawns on the opposite color complex as his opponent's bishop so that the opponent can't target the pawns in the end game. But it makes sense because in the middle game pawns should defend pawns and pawns on the opposite color of our bishop means that we are not restricting our bishops mobility while at the same time are restricting the opponent's bishop's mobility.} 13. Nxd6+ {[%clk 0:03:15] } Qxd6 {[%clk 0:02:18] On h4 we would probably play h6. The f, g pawn structure is important because we potentially have a home for our LSB on f5 which can't be harassed by the knight. So it makes sense for our opponent to challenge it with h4.} 14. Qc3 {[%clk 0:03:01] } b6 {[%clk 0:02:00] Aman considered Bg4 with Bxf3 ideas seemingly interested in a N vs B end game where he has the N. But in the end he goes for the previous plan of placing pawns on dark squares while he has the LSB vs DSB. Kd7 planned as a follow up.} 15. h4 {[%clk 0:02:49] } h6 {[%clk 0:01:51] If we advanced g4, the knight moves and our opponent plays g3 and Bf4 which we don't love. If our opponent takes hxg5 we would not be interested in fxg5 because that would give the knight the e5 square. (so presumably Aman would recapture hxg5 offering the rook trade).} 16. g3 {[%clk 0:02:44] Aman believes this move to be a mistake. Our opponent is placing pawns on the same color as his bishop, vs we who are putting ours on the opposite color. It means the light squares are going to become very weak. So Aman's prediction at the start of the game seems to be coming true here.} Bg4 {[%clk 0:01:39] Look at the mobility of our bishop vs the opponent's. We can traverse the board at will. Our opponent is boxed in and restricted.} 17. Qa3 {[%clk 0:02:12] loses on the spot. The opponent should have played Bd2 indirectly defending the knight with the queen.} Qxa3 {[%clk 0:01:33] } 18. bxa3 {[%clk 0:02:09] } Bxf3 {[%clk 0:01:32] Brutal as this also forks the exchange.} 19. hxg5 {[%clk 0:02:01] } Bxd1 {[%clk 0:01:24] } 20. Rxd1 {[%clk 0:01:59] } hxg5 {[%clk 0:01:22] } 21. f4 {[%clk 0:01:54] } g4 {[%clk 0:01:18] Aman comments here that c4 is an important square in the French. We see how it could become a strong outpost for our knight via Na5, Nc4. Especially strong in a N vs DSB end game as our knight would be anchored on a light square. (e4 and f5 would also be decent outposts)} 22. a4 {[%clk 0:01:31] } Rh3 {[%clk 0:01:10] Aman considered Na5 but decided to start by putting some pressure on the weak pawn.} 23. Rg1 {[%clk 0:01:24] } Re8 {[%clk 0:01:06] Hitting the bishop and then if Kd2 to defend our knight maneuver would then come with a check and tempo.} 24. Bf2 {[%clk 0:01:16] } Rh2 {[%clk 0:00:55] Aman considered Re2 here but decided this was stronger. Notice the bishop is running out of squares. Re2 as a follow up seems to win the bishop.} 25. a3 {[%clk 0:01:10] } Rxf2 {[%clk 0:00:53] with ideas of Re2} 26. Rh1 {[%clk 0:01:00] } Ree2 {[%clk 0:00:51] } 27. Rh8+ {[%clk 0:00:55] spite check.} Kb7 {[%clk 0:00:50] } 28. Rf8 {[%clk 0:00:52] } Rxc2+ {[%clk 0:00:46] with plans to mate. We now have forced mate in 4.} 29. Kd1 {[%clk 0:00:49] } Ra2 {[%clk 0:00:44] } 30. Ke1 {[%clk 0:00:45] } Rh2 {[%clk 0:00:42] mate now can't be stopped, only delayed with Rb8+.} 31. Kf1 {[%clk 0:00:43] } Ra1# {[%clk 0:00:42] 0-1 Black wins.} 0-1

