{ https://youtu.be/C3h3t6GxTH8?si=xEFABwb6ZuquoyDj&t=1243 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:53] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3. e5 { [%clk 0:04:51] } c5 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 4. c3 { [%clk 0:04:45] a rule of thumb is that if the opponent does not play c3 we should take cxd4. } Qb6 { [%clk 0:04:24] Putting pressure on the d4 pawn but also on the b2 pawn in the case the Bishop moves. Interchangeable with 4. Nc6. Aman will play the Nc6 move first more at the higher elos. At the lower ELOs he anticipates more players will blunder by moving the bishop out here and dropping the b2 pawn immediately.
At higher ELOs a reason to bring out the queen first like this might be to play Bd7, Bb5 to trade off our bad French bishop early. } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:34] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:19] } 6. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:32] seemingly drops the d pawn but we need to be careful of discovered attacks on the d file coming with check due to Bb5. } Bd7 { [%clk 0:04:11] Covers any Bb5 discovered attack with check ideas. } 7. Be3 { [%clk 0:04:15] Dropping the b2 pawn. Aman comments earlier in the match that lower ELO players playing the French advanced find it very difficult to keep their pawn chain intact as they don't know the accurate way to defend and often collapse to queen side pressure. } Qxb2 { [%clk 0:04:01] } 8. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:03:49] } Qxc3 { [%clk 0:03:50] In later games Aman will play c4 first here before taking the pawn. } 9. O-O?? { [%clk 0:03:49] The opponent seemingly pre-moved this. Drops the undefended LSB. } Qxd3 { [%clk 0:03:46] } 10. Rb1 { [%clk 0:03:43] } cxd4 { [%clk 0:03:38] white's pawn structure and center continues to collapse } 11. Rb3 { [%clk 0:03:41] } Qa6 { [%clk 0:03:19] Aman considered some queen sac ideas but staying true to form chose a principled retreat. } 12. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:03:40] } Nxd4 { [%clk 0:03:16] } 13. Bxd4 { [%clk 0:03:39] We don'd mind giving a pawn back for the simplification } Ne7 { [%clk 0:03:14] 0-1 Black wins. continuing to develop. Plans for Nc6. } 0-1

