{ https://youtu.be/C3h3t6GxTH8?si=NJDOD6rcAZ4yiQQg&t=1462 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:58] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 3. e5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } c5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 4. c3 { [%clk 0:04:48] Again, if our opponent does not play c3 here we will probably take cxd4. } Qb6 { [%clk 0:04:49] Aman mentions again that starting with Nc6 is possible but he is starting with Qb6 to leave the option open to play Bd7, Bb5 to trade off the bad French bishop. } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:39] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:47] } 6. Be3 { [%clk 0:04:31] And again we see b2 blundered. } Qxb2 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 7. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:11] } c4 { [%clk 0:04:10] Aman plays the c4 move first here. Interestingly it appears weaker than the previous game when the c4 move came with tempo on the LSB which was on d3, but in this position the engine much prefers c4 first and the evaluation is -2.6 after playing it. Aman was worried about Rc3 winning a tempo on the queen after taking the pawn and the engine seems to agree that we would lose some tempii for the pawn.
This move stops our opponent's development and his c3 pawn is still hanging. On Rc1 we will the a2 pawn and on Qc1 we have Ba3 } 8. Be2 { [%clk 0:03:48] Stronger was Rc3. } (8. Rc1 Qxa2) (8. Qc1 Ba3) 8... Qxc3 { [%clk 0:04:03] } 9. O-O { [%clk 0:03:46] Hambo pawn grabbing again! } Nh6 { [%clk 0:03:50] Ne7 (to get to f5) also fine. But Nh6 leaves the DSB open in case we need to move it to a3. Since we are 2 pawns up it is unlikely that our opponent will want to trade by playing Bxh6. } 10. Bxh6 { [%clk 0:03:33] He trades anyway. We don't mind as this simplifies, leaves us with the bishop pair, and also the d4 pawn is now weak } gxh6 { [%clk 0:03:49] } 11. Rc1 { [%clk 0:03:17] } Qa3 { [%clk 0:03:28] Aman also considered a5 here, which the engine actually prefers.
If our opponent sacs Bxc4, dxc4, Nxc4 our bishop is covering d6.
We can't wait to play b5 here. } 12. Rc2 { [%clk 0:03:01] } (12. Bxc4 dxc4 13. Nxc4 Qa6 14. Nd6+ Bxd6 15. exd6) 12... b5 { [%clk 0:03:25] solidifies everything. We don't plan to rush Nd4 here. } 13. Nb1 { [%clk 0:02:58] } Qa5 { [%clk 0:03:18] On Nc3, b4. Next we want to get the LSB developed. Probably Bd7 eying the a4 square. } 14. a3 { [%clk 0:02:45] We can play Bxa3 but then Ra2 comes and we don't want to deal with that. } Bd7 { [%clk 0:03:10] } 15. Nh4 { [%clk 0:02:41] } Be7 { [%clk 0:03:05] Asking our opponent what his intentions are. } 16. Nf3 { [%clk 0:02:32] } Rg8 { [%clk 0:02:51] Rook on an open file, in front of the king. No calculation required. Also, Rg6 ideas. (protecting the h6 pawn). Rb8 or Rc8 follow up ideas. We've not castled, but one good aspect of the French is that the center is closed so there is often not a pressing need to do so. } 17. Rb2 { [%clk 0:01:57] } a6 { [%clk 0:02:39] Start with a6 because we might want to O-O-O so don't want to commit our rook just yet. } 18. Qc1 { [%clk 0:01:49] hitting the h6 pawn. } Rg6 { [%clk 0:02:30] Defending the pawn. } 19. g3 { [%clk 0:01:41] Maybe planning Nh4? } Bxa3 { [%clk 0:02:06] This is now playable because ion Ra2 we can take the queen with Bxc1 } 20. Nxa3 { [%clk 0:01:36] } Qxa3 { [%clk 0:02:06] Now we have 3 connected passed pawns. } 21. Qc2 { [%clk 0:01:24] } b4 { [%clk 0:01:26] Aman decided Ra2 was not a threat. (we will meet it with b3 threatening the queen. Although after playing the move Aman noticed Qxg6 there. Engine seems to think it's fine.) On Nh4 we don't care about the rook, we will be taking Nxd4 with an attack on the queen. O-O-O not desirable because of all of the open lines to our queenside. Kf8 an option. We are playing b4 with b3 ideas. } 22. Qb1 { [%clk 0:01:01] } (22. Ra2 b3 23. Qxg6 bxa2 24. Qg8+ Qf8 25. Qxh7 Rb8 26. Ra1 Rb2) 22... b3 { [%clk 0:01:19] Pushing the passed pawns, and creating an escape for our queen to prevent it getting trapped. } 23. h4 { [%clk 0:00:54] On h5 we will just drop the rook back. } Rb8 { [%clk 0:01:10] Supporting the passed b pawn. Now c3 is an idea. } 24. h5 { [%clk 0:00:51] } Rg7 { [%clk 0:01:08] Qc1 to hit the pawn nothing to worry about at the queen is tied down defending the rook. } 25. Nd2 { [%clk 0:00:30] c3 can be met with Rxb3 } Nxd4 { [%clk 0:00:49] } (25... c3 26. Rxb3 Rxb3 27. Qxb3 Qxb3 28. Nxb3) 26. Bxc4 { [%clk 0:00:11] Aman was expecting this sac, but doesn't think it works. } dxc4 { [%clk 0:00:44] } 27. Nxc4 { [%clk 0:00:09] } Qc5 { [%clk 0:00:41] } 28. Rc1 { [%clk 0:00:05] Aman was expecting Nb6+ on which we would have simply played Kf8. } Kf8 { [%clk 0:00:22] "A nice safe move". Aman looked at some tactical shots, rook sacs etc., but who wants to deal with all of those messy complications? } 29. Nd6 { [%clk 0:00:02] } Qxe5 { [%clk 0:00:20] 0-1 Black wins on time. } 0-1

