:04] developing with tempo } 17. Na3 { [%clk 0:03:08] spot the tactic.... } Qa5+ { [%clk 0:02:01] and we have a puzzle rush like fork on the undefended knight. } 18. Nd2 { [%clk 0:02:59] } Qxa3 { [%clk 0:01:58] } 19. O-O { [%clk 0:02:55] } Be7 { [%clk 0:01:48] } 20. Ne4 { [%clk 0:02:50] } O-O { [%clk 0:01:42] } 21. Rad1 { [%clk 0:02:47] } Bc6 { [%clk 0:01:40] } 22. Rfe1 { [%clk 0:02:27] } Qxa2 { [%clk 0:01:33] pawn grabber Hambo can't resist. To be fair we also have the threat of Qg2# should our opponent move the knight. } 23. Re2 { [%clk 0:02:14] } Qa3 { [%clk 0:01:21] Defending the c pawn. } 24. Rg2 { [%clk 0:01:52] } Bxe4 { [%clk 0:01:14] Simplifying but also threatening to deflect the queen from defense of the b pawn. } 25. Qxe4 { [%clk 0:01:41] } Qxb3 { [%clk 0:01:12] And Pawn Grabber Hambo reaches ecstasy. Also forks the rook and h pawn. } 26. Qd3 { [%clk 0:01:20] } Qxd3 { [%clk 0:01:07] } 27. Rxd3 { [%clk 0:01:18] } Rfd8 { [%clk 0:01:06] } 28. Re3 { [%clk 0:01:16] } Rd7 { [%clk 0:00:56] Aman considers Rd4 here but decides on Rd2. Maybe to defend the Bishop? Or be ready for defense of the second rank/ king? Probably not needed since Bf8 should be enough to defend against the rooks doubling on the g file. } 29. Kh2 { [%clk 0:01:14] } Rad8 { [%clk 0:00:55] } 30. Reg3 { [%clk 0:01:10] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:00:53] Pinning the rook to the king. Brutal. } 31. Kh1 { [%clk 0:01:03] } Bxg3 { [%clk 0:00:53] } 32. Rxg3 { [%clk 0:01:02] } Rd3 { [%clk 0:00:51] } 33. Kg2 { [%clk 0:01:01] } Rxg3+ { [%clk 0:00:50] } 34. Kxg3 { [%clk 0:01:01] } Rd4 { [%clk 0:00:49] } 35. Kf3 { [%clk 0:00:59] } h5 { [%clk 0:00:49] } 36. Ke3 { [%clk 0:00:59] } g5 { [%clk 0:00:48] } 37. Kf3 { [%clk 0:00:55] } f5 { [%clk 0:00:47] } 38. h4 { [%clk 0:00:53] } Rxh4 { [%clk 0:00:46] } 39. Ke3 { [%clk 0:00:52] } Rd4 { [%clk 0:00:45] And from here Aman proceeds to play with his food. } 40. Kf3 { [%clk 0:00:49] } e5 { [%clk 0:00:45] } 41. Ke3 { [%clk 0:00:48] } b5 { [%clk 0:00:44] } 42. Kf3 { [%clk 0:00:48] } 