1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] https://youtu.be/C3h3t6GxTH8?si=rEjCyeI9xomXMQYJ&t=3203 } e6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 3. e5 { [%clk 0:04:51] } c5 { [%clk 0:04:43] } 4. c3 { [%clk 0:04:49] } Qb6 { [%clk 0:04:41] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:46] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:38] } 6. Be3 { [%clk 0:04:37] oh dear. } Qxb2 { [%clk 0:04:33] } 7. Nbd2 { [%clk 0:04:10] } c4 { [%clk 0:04:26] c4 first. The engine gives this as significantly better. } 8. Rb1 { [%clk 0:04:07] } Qxc3 { [%clk 0:04:24] Qxc3 and not Qxa2. Probably because it's better to take a central pawn, better to undermine the central pawn chain, and the a pawn can actually provide us cover should our queen come under attack by the rook and need to retreat. } 9. Be2 { [%clk 0:03:54] } Nge7 { [%clk 0:04:13] With f5 ideas next move so Aman probably doesn't mind blocking in his DSB. He has played Nh6 here previously but maybe found the doubled pawns annoying to defend so is keeping things more simple in this game? Or actually in the other game Ba3 was a strong move in response to our opponent offering a queen trade and in this case since that move is not available it is not as important to keep the diagonal open for the bishop. } 10. O-O { [%clk 0:03:52] } Nf5 { [%clk 0:04:10] threatening Nxe3, fxe3, Qxe3 winning a pawn. } 11. Bg5 { [%clk 0:03:44] } h6 { [%clk 0:04:01] Kick the bishop. d4 pawn is hanging and can be taken anytime. } 12. Bf4 { [%clk 0:03:37] } Nfxd4 { [%clk 0:03:10] Aman considered captures with Nfx and Ncx. The knight on c6 is blocking a queen check so Aman wanted to keep it there. (chat pointed out Bd7 to defend in that case but Aman countered that that would leave the b pawn loose to capture Rxb7) He also liked that the knight on f5 covered the g3 and e3 squares, and in the case he played g5 he would be able to win the bishop retreating to those squares. } 13. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:03:25] } Qxd4 { [%clk 0:03:06] } 14. Be3 { [%clk 0:03:10] } Qxe5 { [%clk 0:03:03] And once again we see an opponent's pawn chain decimated in the French advanced. } 15. Nf3 { [%clk 0:03:05] } Qc7 { [%clk 0:02:51] } 16. Re1 { [%clk 0:02:53] } Be7 { [%clk 0:02:49] with O-O ideas next move. } 17. Bc1 { [%clk 0:02:43] with Bc1, Bb2 ideas? Aman considers this to be an interesting move. The bishop would be strong on that diagonal. Taking advantages of the imbalances! Like Silman says, you need to find out what is advantageous for you in the imbalances in your position. Losing all your pawns will be advantageous to your opponent in the end game, and in supporting minor pieces taking up outposts in your position, and (in this case) of restricting mobility of your LSB, and in denying your rooks open files, BUT! you do get a nice long diagonal to the opponent's king, so why not take advantage of that?! } O-O { [%clk 0:02:36] } 18. Nd4 { [%clk 0:02:38] } Nxd4 { [%clk 0:02:31] } 19. Qxd4 { [%clk 0:02:38] our opponent probably aiming to swindle the game with this queen recapture followed by Bb2 followed by Qxg7#. } Bf6 { [%clk 0:02:26] But we deny this cheapo. Qg4, Aman plans a5. It would look like he missed a Bxa6 idea but it discovers an attack on our opponent's queen by the LSB. } 20. Qg4 { [%clk 0:02:28] } e5 { [%clk 0:02:26] } 21. Bb2 { [%clk 0:02:23] That's not it. } Bxg4 { [%clk 0:02:18] 0-1 Black wins. Played with Aman's evil laugh. Brutal. } 0-1

