{ https://youtu.be/EBEuKTqShpE?si=ifkvTrNZylm33iXD&t=10 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:54] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 3. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:51] Exchange but the early Qe2 might put us in the French even if we started intending the Stonewall. } exd5 { [%clk 0:04:53] } 4. Qe2+ { [%clk 0:04:51] } Be7 { [%clk 0:04:49] In the game against nahcohen Aman played Ne2 but that was with whites pawn on d4 and our DSB already developed. } 5. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:49] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:04:47] Need to defend the pawn so the stonewall continuation of f5 or Bd6 or whatever would seem ill advised. (Although the queen is protecting the pawn.).
Here we just want to get castled ASAP and get our rook on that e file. } (5... f5 6. d4 Nf6 7. Qd1 O-O 8. Bg5 Bd6 (8... c6 9. Bd3 Bd6 10. O-O Bd7 11. Ne5 Qc7 12. Bxf6 Rxf6 { SW might be possible with this kind of line. }) 9. Bxf6 Qxf6 10. Nxd5 { wonestall ideas possible but we always need to be mindful of this pawn. }) 6. d4 { [%clk 0:04:44] } O-O { [%clk 0:04:45] } 7. Na4 { [%clk 0:04:19] Took Aman by surprise. "wow, I don't think I could have predicted that". } Bb4+ { [%clk 0:03:48] If we played Re8 here our opponent would know what we were up to and probably play Be3, straight away or later. He could play c3 in response but then we'll just take a step back (and maybe argue compensation that we have weakened the light squares?). He could play Nc3 but Aman is banking on him not finding this since backwards night moves are hard to spot and sunk cost fallacy means people are reluctant to move pieces back to where they have just come from. If he plays Bd2 we can then proceed to play Re8 to attack that queen pinned to the king. (Although our opponent does seem to have Ne5 in response which Aman does not discuss here). } 8. Bd2 { [%clk 0:04:14] in the analysis Aman clarifies that on c3 he would have played Bd6. } Re8 { [%clk 0:03:37] } 9. Bxb4 { [%clk 0:04:11] } Rxe2+ { [%clk 0:03:28] } 10. Bxe2 { [%clk 0:04:11] Queen for a piece and a rook so not that amazing. But of course black is winning here. } Qe8 { [%clk 0:02:53] Stopping our opponent castling. Although after Nc3 it's not that special. Positionally, though, Aman thinks the Bishop is supposed to be "on the inside there" so could become a weakness. } 11. Nc3 { [%clk 0:03:59] } a5 { [%clk 0:02:50] immediately attacking that loose bishop. } 12. Ba3 { [%clk 0:03:50] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:02:46] We are living on the edge here. On O-O, Nxc3, bxc3, Qxe2 winning a piece, BUT! Re1 skewers back rank mate and wins the queen. However, here we have Qb5! and we are saving the queen while covering the e8 square. } 13. O-O-O { [%clk 0:03:31] } Nxc3 { [%clk 0:02:42] } 14. bxc3 { [%clk 0:03:27] } Qxe2 { [%clk 0:02:38] } 15. Rd2 { [%clk 0:03:23] Perhaps our opponent didn't see the skewered back rank. Or perhaps they say all the way to the Qb5! resource. } Qc4 { [%clk 0:02:23] Forking c3 and a2. } 16. Re1 { [%clk 0:03:20] } Be6 { [%clk 0:02:17] Denying the rook infiltration (back rank mate actually) with a developing move. We have Nc6 ideas next meaning the rook on a8 will cover the back rank. We are not afraid of Ng5. } 17. Rde2 { [%clk 0:03:02] still does not to be any threat for now. } Qxa2 { [%clk 0:02:07] Qxc3 there was Bb2 in response. } 18. Bb2 { [%clk 0:02:55] more or less forced. (as the bishop was hanging). } a4 { [%clk 0:02:04] with a3 ideas winning the trapped bishop. } 19. Ng5 { [%clk 0:02:53] } a3 { [%clk 0:02:01] } 20. Bxa3 { [%clk 0:02:45] } Qxa3+ { [%clk 0:01:58] } 21. Kd2 { [%clk 0:02:42] } Nd7 { [%clk 0:01:55] completing development. } 22. Nxe6 { [%clk 0:02:40] } fxe6 { [%clk 0:01:54] } 23. Rxe6 { [%clk 0:02:39] } Nb6 { [%clk 0:01:50] with Nc4+ ideas. } 24. R6e5 { [%clk 0:02:31] } Nc4+ { [%clk 0:01:50] } 25. Kd3 { [%clk 0:02:28] } Nxe5+ { [%clk 0:01:48] } 26. Rxe5 { [%clk 0:02:26] } Qc1 { [%clk 0:01:43] } 27. Rxd5 { [%clk 0:02:23] } Qf1+ { [%clk 0:01:39] stop the king from escaping via c4 } 28. Ke3 { [%clk 0:02:20] } Re8+ { [%clk 0:01:37] } 29. Kd2 { [%clk 0:02:18] } Qe1+ { [%clk 0:01:32] } 30. Kd3 { [%clk 0:02:15] } Qe2# { [%clk 0:01:31] 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

