{ https://youtu.be/EBEuKTqShpE?si=EGMcfKy0qQgiydsr&t=3057 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:57] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3. d4 { [%clk 0:04:50] It's likely we'd actually play f5 rather than go into the Winawer but it transposes to advanced from here so leaving it. } Bb4 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 4. e5 { [%clk 0:04:47] } Ne7 { [%clk 0:04:27] Here c5 is the main line but we are going for this sideline which Aman likes. The idea is we take the knight on c3 regardless of whether it doubles pawns or not. Another plan is b6 followed by Ba6 with the idea to trade off our bad French bishop and recapture with Na6 which will then come back via Nb8 before going to Nc6 where it will eye landing on the c4 square via Na5, Nc4. Our other knight will go to Nf5 and we will castle king side. } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:35] Nf3 is nice for us because it means we don't have to deal with Qg4 attacking our g7 pawn. Qg4 is a nice counter to Bb4 for white. } b6 { [%clk 0:03:54] } 6. a3 { [%clk 0:04:25] } Bxc3+ { [%clk 0:03:52] } 7. bxc3 { [%clk 0:04:25] } Ba6 { [%clk 0:03:52] } 8. Bxa6 { [%clk 0:04:18] } Nxa6 { [%clk 0:03:52] } 9. O-O { [%clk 0:04:18] } Nb8 { [%clk 0:03:48] Everything is going to plan so far. We are looking to play positional chess here. We are going to be weak on the dark squares but strong on the light squares. (our opponent has a DSB and we don't. We are controlling lots of light squares via our central pawns which will be outposts for our knights). } 10. Be3 { [%clk 0:04:12] } Nbc6 { [%clk 0:03:46] } 11. Qe2 { [%clk 0:04:05] } Na5 { [%clk 0:03:39] } 12. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:01] A common plan for white is to move this knight like this and play f4. Aman explains that instead of playing g6 to stop that usually you want to play h5. "doesn't make a lot of sense, but that's normally what the plan is". OK, thanks Aman. We will have to hope it becomes clearer later in the video.
Later in the video Aman responds to a question from chat clarifying that on f4, h5, with our knight on f5 our opponent cannot play g4 to book the knight. } Qd7 { [%clk 0:03:27] } 13. c4 { [%clk 0:03:54] } dxc4 { [%clk 0:03:08] We take this and anticipate going into a NvB end game. Given most of our pawns are fixed on light squares and our opponent's fixed on dark squares we are comfortable with this outcome. } 14. Nxc4 { [%clk 0:03:54] } Nxc4 { [%clk 0:03:05] } 15. Qxc4 { [%clk 0:03:53] } O-O { [%clk 0:03:04] } 16. Rfb1 { [%clk 0:03:43] } Rfd8 { [%clk 0:02:57] } 17. c3 { [%clk 0:03:37] } h6 { [%clk 0:02:52] Escape square. h6 because our opponent doesn't have a LSB to clog up our snork. Plans for c5 as a follow up, but we need to find the right moment to do so. Possibly Rc8, c5. Also Nd5 and if the bishop moves c5 becomes more attainable.
For our opponent, a4, a5 looks strong. } 18. Qd3 { [%clk 0:02:47] } c5 { [%clk 0:02:49] Qd3 allows c5. } 19. Rc1 { [%clk 0:02:40] } cxd4 { [%clk 0:02:46] After trading the c pawns, the d5 square becomes available as a permanent outpost for our knight. } 20. cxd4 { [%clk 0:02:36] } Rac8 { [%clk 0:02:44] A dream position. We have an outside passed pawn. (or a queenside pawn majority that will become one). } 21. Bd2 { [%clk 0:02:15] Removing a defender from the d pawn. } Rxc1+ { [%clk 0:02:42] Simplification helps us as we have the outside passed pawn. Also we can win the d pawn shortly with further simplifications. Aman initially pre-moved Rdc8 but spotted the tactic to win the pawn due to the opponent taking a while to decide on the best recapture of the rook. (bishop or rook. Either way it seems the tactic was available to win the pawn) } 22. Rxc1 { [%clk 0:01:42] } Qxd4 { [%clk 0:02:40] } 23. Qxd4 { [%clk 0:01:18] } Rxd4 { [%clk 0:02:40] } 24. Bb4 { [%clk 0:01:17] } Nd5 { [%clk 0:02:34] } 25. Be1 { [%clk 0:01:06] } Re4 { [%clk 0:02:25] } 26. Bd2 { [%clk 0:01:01] } Rxe5 { [%clk 0:02:22] } 27. Rc8+ { [%clk 0:00:55] } Kh7 { [%clk 0:02:21] } 28. Ra8 { [%clk 0:00:54] } Re2 { [%clk 0:02:19] 0-1 Black wins. Bishop needs to watch over the e1 square to avoid mate but all of the retreat squares are covered on that diagonal meaning loss of material cannot be avoided here. } 0-1

