a6 was also an option but stopping the threat with a developing move, while putting pressure on the opponent's b pawn, is both more flexible and accomplishes more with a single move. } 12. Rb1 { [%clk 0:04:30] } Be7 { [%clk 0:03:14] Continuing with development. } 13. a4 { [%clk 0:04:29] Probably trying to create a support point for a Nb5 jump. Notice this weakens the b4 square. } a6 { [%clk 0:02:40] Now we play a6 to completely shut down Nb5 ideas. As Silman says, you beat knights by denying them advanced support points.
