{ https://youtu.be/dissPDPzWas?si=rOkYNKsytu3Fq218&t=1505 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } (1... d5 { As a Stonewall player you would respond d5 here. }) 2. e4 { [%clk 0:04:56] transposing to the French. } d5 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 3. e5 { [%clk 0:04:54] The advanced. } c5 { [%clk 0:04:36] } 4. c3 { [%clk 0:04:48] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:31] I think this is the first game Aman went with Nc6 before Qb6. Perhaps trusting a 1400 not to immediately blunder the b2 pawn. } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:44] } Qb6 { [%clk 0:04:29] } 6. Qd2 { [%clk 0:04:29] "definitely a bad move. There is nothing good about that move". Takes the knights square, blocks the bishop. } Bd7 { [%clk 0:04:09] We can't take d4 because there are 3 attackers and 3 defenders so we'd need to add more pressure. Aman plans Rc8 as a follow up. Not sure about the Rc8 move as I don't think we've seen this before and Aman doesn't explain why it's an idea here. I assume that we expect the c file to open up. } (6... cxd4 7. cxd4 Nxd4 (7... Nh6 8. Bd3 Nf5 (8... Bd7 9. Bc2 Nb4 10. Bd1) 9. Bxf5 exf5) 8. Nxd4 Qxd4 9. Qxd4) 7. Be2 { [%clk 0:04:28] } Nh6 { [%clk 0:03:07] Not sure what happened to the Rc8 idea but Aman hasn't mentioned it again. The nice thing about the queen being on d2 is that we can play Nh6 to get to Nf5 without worrying about Bxh6. Aman also considered cxd4, cxd4, Bb4 pinning the queen. White does have Nc3, though, so we wouldn't be winning the queen with this move (at least not right away). } (7... cxd4 8. cxd4 Bb4 9. Nc3 { not quite winning the queen. }) 8. b3 { [%clk 0:04:05] Aman thought white may play Na3 here looking to re-route to Nc2 to add a defender to the c pawn. } cxd4 { [%clk 0:02:44] } 9. cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:00] } Nf5 { [%clk 0:02:39] threatening the d pawn (3 attackers vs 2 defenders) } 10. Bb2 { [%clk 0:03:55] } Bb4 { [%clk 0:02:38] the idea is that whatever white does here it probably leads to dropping the d pawn. } 11. Nc3 { [%clk 0:03:52] blocking the bishop's defense. } Nfxd4 { [%clk 0:02:27] Aman takes with this knight because the knight on c6 is well placed attacking the e pawn. So if our opponent castles or something we could play Nxf3, Bxf3, Nxe5 winning the e pawn. } 12. a3?? { [%clk 0:03:38] blundering b3 } Nxb3 { [%clk 0:02:20] comes with tempo (an attack on the queen) and is a fork of the queen and rook. } 13. Qc2 { [%clk 0:03:24] } Nxa1 { [%clk 0:02:04] } 14. Bxa1 { [%clk 0:03:24] } Bxa3 { [%clk 0:01:57] Pawn grabber Hambo can't help himself. } 15. O-O { [%clk 0:03:22] } Be7 { [%clk 0:01:41] Keeps the knight out of g5 } 16. Rb1 { [%clk 0:03:16] } Nb4 { [%clk 0:01:34] with ideas to play a5 to support the knight which cannot be challenged by any pawns. } 17. Qd2 { [%clk 0:02:52] } a5 { [%clk 0:01:31] The knight is defended by who pieces (the bishop and queen) but it's good practice to anchor it with a pawn in this way to free the pieces up to move away if needed. (the concept of overprotection) } 18. Nd4 { [%clk 0:02:32] This knight is going nowhere } Rc8 { [%clk 0:01:19] and now we see the rook come to the open c file. } 19. Ncb5 { [%clk 0:02:24] } O-O { [%clk 0:01:12] Bxb5 would be met by Bxb5+ and would have been uncomfortable. Prioritizing king safety. } 20. Bc3 { [%clk 0:02:16] we're not concerned about Bxb4 } f6 { [%clk 0:00:44] Looking to "accelerate the opening of the position". If exf6, we will take back Bxf6. That would drop a pawn (Bxb4, axb4, Qxb4) but it would help in terms of simplifying down into a winning end game. } 21. Nd6 { [%clk 0:01:59] } Rxc3 { [%clk 0:00:37] giving back the exchange but after fxe5 we have a good position according to Aman. Engine also has this as the top move. It is something to do with the fact two knights will be hanging after the dust settles as well as the threat of Qxf2 (as the fxe5 moves opens up the f file) so at a minimum we should win a knight in addition to the bishop so two pieces (and a pawn) for the rook. } 22. Qxc3 { [%clk 0:01:52] } fxe5 { [%clk 0:00:35] two knight now hanging. We are winning 2 pieces for the rook. } 23. N4b5 { [%clk 0:01:37] } Qxf2+ { [%clk 0:00:26] will win the bishop after this fork. } 24. Kh1 { [%clk 0:01:27] } Qxe2 { [%clk 0:00:26] threatening Rf1+, Rxf1, Qxf1# and also Bxb5. } 25. Rg1 { [%clk 0:01:13] stops mate but of course the knight is still hanging. } Bxb5 { [%clk 0:00:23] } 26. Nxb5 { [%clk 0:01:08] } Qxb5 { [%clk 0:00:22] } 27. Qxe5 { [%clk 0:01:05] } Qd7 { [%clk 0:00:19] } 28. h3 { [%clk 0:01:00] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:00:18] we're covering the h2 square here so there are real threats. } 29. Qd4 { [%clk 0:00:55] } e5 { [%clk 0:00:16] wins a tempo on the queen while also preparing a support point for the knight and after e5, Nd3, Nf2# } 30. Qb6 { [%clk 0:00:52] } e4 { [%clk 0:00:15] } 31. Qd4 { [%clk 0:00:50] } Nd3 { [%clk 0:00:14] } 32. Ra1 { [%clk 0:00:43] saves mate. } Be5 { [%clk 0:00:10] skewering the rook. } 33. Qa4 { [%clk 0:00:39] } Nf2+ { [%clk 0:00:08] } 34. Kg1 { [%clk 0:00:36] } Qxa4 { [%clk 0:00:07] } 35. Rxa4 { [%clk 0:00:34] } d4 { [%clk 0:00:07] } 36. Rxa5 { [%clk 0:00:29] } d3 { [%clk 0:00:07] } 37. Ra2 { [%clk 0:00:27] } e3 { [%clk 0:00:06] } 38. Ra1 { [%clk 0:00:24] } d2 { [%clk 0:00:05] } 39. Rb1 { [%clk 0:00:23] } e2 { [%clk 0:00:04] } 40. Rf1 { [%clk 0:00:20] } e1=Q { [%clk 0:00:04] } 41. Rxe1 { [%clk 0:00:19] } dxe1=Q# { [%clk 0:00:04] 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

