ning as we should be able to convert our kingside 3-1 majority into a passed pawn. }) 21... Bc6 { [%clk 0:03:41] } 22. Rd4 { [%clk 0:03:05] } g5 { [%clk 0:03:34] } 23. f4 { [%clk 0:03:00] } f6 { [%clk 0:03:32] } 24. fxg5 { [%clk 0:02:53] } hxg5 { [%clk 0:03:29] Aman explains here that in an opposite color bishop end game you generally want to advance your pawns on the same color complex as THE OPPONENT'S bishop. (this is the same as same colored bishop end games where you want to be able to control the square in front of the advanced pawn with your bishop to avoid the opponent blockading you with the king, for example. For opposite colored bishops the principle must be similar in that we want to control the color in front of the advanced pawn to clear blockades. ) } 25. Rd1 { [%clk 0:02:39] } Kf7 { [%clk 0:03:25] } (25... f5 26. Rd2 g4 27. Bf4 { this would be a mistake as the pawns cannot advance. } (27. Bg5 { or even stronger according to the engine. })) 26. Kg3 { [%clk 0:02:38] } Kg6 { [%clk 0:03:23] } 27. b4 { [%clk 0:02:31] } f5 { [%clk 0:03:21] } 28. Kf2 { [%clk 0:02:23] } f4 { [%clk 0:03:18] pawns controlling dark squares, bishops controlling light squares, supported by the king. } 29. Bc1 { [%clk 0:02:19] } Kf5 { [%clk 0:03:15] } 30. Re1 { [%clk 0:02:15] } g4 { [%clk 0:03:13] } 31. Bb2 { [%clk 0:02:13] } g3+ { [%clk 0:03:12] } 32. Kg1 { [%clk 0:02:10] } f3 { [%clk 0:03:10] } 33. Re5+ { [%clk 0:01:57] } Kf4 { [%clk 0:03:03] } 34. Re7 { [%clk 0:01:38] } f2+ { [%clk 0:03:01] } 35. Kf1 { [%clk 0:01:34] } Bb5+ { [%clk 0:02:57] } 36. Re2 { [%clk 0:01:18] } Kf3 { [%clk 0:02:54] 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

