{ https://youtu.be/mdLIVFuqMTI?si=VI0qrryM_3JH9-43&t=635 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 3. e5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } c5 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 4. c3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:38] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } Qb6 { [%clk 0:04:36] } 6. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:55] } Bd7 { [%clk 0:04:34] } 7. O-O { [%clk 0:04:53] Our opponent is hanging the d4 pawn, but according to Aman this is theory. Indeed the engine has this position slightly better for white. } cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:25] Now the engine has an equal evaluation after the initial capture. } 8. cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:52] The top engine response was Re1. It seems this natural recapture is leaving white slightly worse. } Nxd4 { [%clk 0:04:24] } 9. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:04:50] This is a mistake. The engine wanted white to develop his b1 knight to d2. However, the position is sharp and black has to be very careful here, underdeveloped and with the queen in a vulnerable position following the recapture. } Qxd4 { [%clk 0:04:21] Engine evaluation -0.5. Not quite a full pawn as white must have some slight compensation. } 10. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:48] It seems our opponent wants to sack the e pawn (in addition to the earlier sacked d pawn) to gain initiative with Re1 attacking our queen? } a6 { [%clk 0:03:33] On Nd7, Nb5 becomes a strong move in response, threatening Nd6+. If you take it Bxb5, you fall to Bxb5+ with a discovered attack on your queen. Aman plays a6 here to prevent all of that. } (10... Qxe5 11. Re1 Qd6 12. Nb5 Qb6 13. Be3 Qa5 14. Bd2 Qb6 15. Be3 Qa5 16. Bd2 { We can see how things can go wrong quickly. The engine evaluation is equal possibly because a repetition of moves is the most likely outcome here? }) (10... Ne7 11. Nb5 Qxe5 (11... Bxb5 12. Bxb5+ Nc6 13. Qxd4 Be7 { A great way to lose your queen. }) 12. Re1 Qb8) 11. Be3?? { [%clk 0:04:41] this move seems to simply blunder the pawn. } Qxe5 { [%clk 0:03:29] Now Re1 is no longer a direct attack on the queen we are able to take? } 12. Re1 { [%clk 0:04:40] Opponent may have ideas such as Nxd5, as pawn recapture would lose the queen to the pin. } Nf6 { [%clk 0:03:02] Defending d5. There don't appear to be any bishop moves (discovering an attack on the queen) which present real threats. } 13. Ne2? { [%clk 0:04:29] } Be7 { [%clk 0:02:38] keeping it simple continuing development. On Bd4, queen can find refuge on h5. } 14. Bf4 { [%clk 0:04:24] } Qh5 { [%clk 0:02:35] Bc6 and O-O follow up ideas, as well as a queen trade should our opponent move the knight (we are up two pawns so would love to simplify). } (14... Qxb2 { More pawn grabbing was available. Aman probably thought two was enough. } 15. Rb1 Qxa2 16. Rxb7 Rc8) 15. Qc2 { [%clk 0:04:14] } O-O { [%clk 0:02:25] h pawn is well defended here against the threat of the bishop queen battery. } 16. Ng3 { [%clk 0:04:10] } Qh4 { [%clk 0:02:20] continuing to defend h7. Rc8 planned as a follow up. } 17. Qd2 { [%clk 0:03:58] threatening Bg5!? } h6 { [%clk 0:01:54] shutting down Bg5 ideas. } 18. h3 { [%clk 0:03:44] } Rac8 { [%clk 0:01:51] } 19. Rac1 { [%clk 0:03:41] } Rxc1 { [%clk 0:01:44] we're happy to trade down. } 20. Rxc1 { [%clk 0:03:41] } Rc8 { [%clk 0:01:43] } 21. Rxc8+ { [%clk 0:03:40] } Bxc8 { [%clk 0:01:43] } 22. Qc3 { [%clk 0:03:34] } Bd7 { [%clk 0:01:14] Aman considered Qxf4, Qxc8+, Kh7 effectively trading bishops (or his good DSB for our bad LSB) but decided against it on account he was worried about losing all of his queenside pawns. } 23. Be3 { [%clk 0:03:32] } Bc6 { [%clk 0:01:08] Blocking off the queen from the 8th rank. } 24. b4 { [%clk 0:03:26] dropping the pawn, attacked by both queen and bishop and only defended by the queen. } Bxb4 { [%clk 0:01:02] A bit of hope chess in that Aman is hoping our opponent doesn't see the sideways queen move and takes Qxb4. The engine prefers Qxb4 offering the queen trade but as sideways queen/ rook moves are often difficult to spot utilization of the dark arts is probably justified in this case. } 25. Qb3 { [%clk 0:03:25] Our opponent doesn't fall for it! } Bd6 { [%clk 0:00:54] } 26. Qb6 { [%clk 0:03:23] threatening Qd8+ picking up the DSB with a fork. } Ne4 { [%clk 0:00:40] The dark magician Hambo again casts a spell--will our opponent see that our knight move indirectly prevents Qd8+ with a discovered defense by the queen? } 27. Nxe4 { [%clk 0:03:17] No! Our brave opponent is impervious to the dark magician's tricks! } dxe4 { [%clk 0:00:37] } 28. Qd8+ { [%clk 0:03:12] "There he is. There's that 1400. He was dying to come out!". } Qxd8 { [%clk 0:00:30] 0-1 Black wins. More black magic from the dark magician as he hovers over the queen capture for a few seconds before taking. He does this quite often and I can only assume it is because he is giving the opponent time to see their mistake and hit the resign button in disgust. Player psychology is more likely to resign before the queen is captured, I think, as it is an impulsive act of the ego to "get in before your opponent" with the resignation, before your queen is captured. If you don't you look like you didn't see it at all, even after the move, making you look more stupid in a way? As it is, the Hannibal Lector of Chess, The Dark Magician Hambo, eventually takes the queen, and our opponent resigns anyway. } 0-1

