{ https://youtu.be/mdLIVFuqMTI?si=OYvcUDpMnXGory4S&t=1097 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:04:56] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:55] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 3. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:53] Tarrasch may force us to consider a continuation in the French.
The Tarrasch variation. More common at higher ELOs. GM Daniel Naroditski (RIP) recommended this against the French https://youtu.be/JKxlT73xpYo?si=V2mfZRGB6f61nkF0 and it was also recommended by Aman in the original Habits series, although in the latest Habits he stuck to the exchange throughout. } c5 { [%clk 0:04:03] Aman has two lines he likes to play against the Tarrasch, neither of which involve capturing the e pawn. Here he plays the "main line" c5. The other line he plays involves playing a6,h6 as "waiting moves", waiting for white to commit to a certain set up before proceeding from there, so maybe we'll get to see that in a later game. } 4. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:27] } Qxd5 { [%clk 0:04:00] Usually we wouldn't play this but in this case we play a developing move, centralize our queen, and there is no knight to jump to c3 to bother it. } 5. dxc5 { [%clk 0:04:25] } Bxc5 { [%clk 0:03:56] On Bc4 we would play Qxg2. } 6. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:25] Preventing Qxg2 and preparing Bc4. } Nf6 { [%clk 0:03:50] } 7. Bc4 { [%clk 0:04:22] } Qd8 { [%clk 0:03:46] In the post game analysis Aman said that Qh5 is the more normal retreat here.
We will see how out opponent puts pressure on our position due to our queen being placed here, on an open file, easily attacked by his rooks.
The engine says Qd8 is fine and is equal to the top move Qh5. } 8. O-O { [%clk 0:04:21] } O-O { [%clk 0:03:45] we have nice easy development. } 9. Qe2 { [%clk 0:04:15] } Nbd7 { [%clk 0:03:26] On b6, we would have to deal with Ne4. Here on Ne4 we can take Nfxe4 (and then replace our knight with Nf3), or we can drop the bishop back Be7. } 10. Nb3 { [%clk 0:04:10] } Be7 { [%clk 0:03:21] as planned. } 11. Bf4 { [%clk 0:04:02] } b6 { [%clk 0:02:58] with Bb7 ideas. } 12. Rad1 { [%clk 0:03:53] } Bb7 { [%clk 0:02:56] On Ne5 we move our queen. On Bd6 we play Bxd6. } 13. Ne5 { [%clk 0:03:40] } Nd5 { [%clk 0:02:07] Aman said he'd move the queen on Ne5 but he then notices Qc8, Nxd7, Nxd7, Bxe6 is "quite concerning", so he switches to play Nd5. On Qe5 Bb5 is a concern.
The engine agrees that Qe5, Bb5 loses (along with Nxd7) but the line is quite complicated. For Qc8, the concern is that after Nxd7, Nxd7, Bxe6 if we recapture the bishop with the f pawn we open ourselves up to Qxe6+ when our bishop is dropping but actually the engine suggests here Ba6! and we are winning in this position with an attack on the queen skewering the exchange.
NB: there is no way for our opponent to play c4 immediately in order to win our knight which would be pinned to the queen on Nxd7, Qxd7. } (13... Qc8 14. Nxd7 Nxd7 15. Bxe6 fxe6 (15... Ba6 16. c4 fxe6 17. Qxe6+ Rf7 18. Qxd7 Qxc4 19. Be3 Ba3 20. Nd2 Qxf1+ 21. Nxf1 Rxd7 22. Rxd7) 16. Qxe6+ Kh8 17. Qxe7) (13... Qe8 14. Bb5 Rd8 15. Nxd7 Nxd7 16. Bc7 a6 17. Bxd8 axb5 18. Bxe7 Qxe7 19. Qxb5 Nf6 20. Qe5 Rc8 21. Qd6 Qe8 22. Qxb6) 14. Bg3 { [%clk 0:03:14] } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:01:59] } 15. Bxe5 { [%clk 0:03:12] } Qe8 { [%clk 0:01:46] moving the queen and unpinning the knight.
On Bb5 we have Bc6 which should hold things together. } 16. Qg4 { [%clk 0:03:02] threatening Qxg7# } Nf6 { [%clk 0:01:24] We need to block the mate threat.
Bf6 loses to Rxd5, Bxe5, Rxe5.
Defending while hitting the queen. Aman pre-moves recapture with the bishop.
We are dreaming of Qc6.
Position is dead even according to the engine. } 17. Nd4 { [%clk 0:02:50] Blundering the queen. Perhaps just seeing Nf6 as blocking the mate without registering the attack on their own queen. } Nxg4 { [%clk 0:01:18] 0-1 Black wins. And despite giving us the best game so far our opponent blunders a full queen to collapse spectacularly. } 0-1

