{ https://youtu.be/mdLIVFuqMTI?si=i-oPztFAOO8vLo15&t=1520 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. Nf3 { [%clk 0:05:00] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:54] } 3. e5 { [%clk 0:04:58] } c5 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 4. c3 { [%clk 0:04:57] Let's just go back to our regular development on c3 } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:48] } 5. h3 { [%clk 0:04:55] } Bd7 { [%clk 0:04:42] We are waiting for d4 for play Qb6. } 6. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:50] a poor move. } Qc7 { [%clk 0:04:19] Hitting the e pawn twice now. } 7. O-O { [%clk 0:04:45] leaves the e pawn hanging. Qe2 was required, to which we would have responded Ne7... Ng6. } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:04:16] } 8. Nxe5 { [%clk 0:04:44] } Qxe5 { [%clk 0:04:16] } 9. Bc2 { [%clk 0:04:40] } Nf6 { [%clk 0:03:57] Here Aman considers some hope chess with Bd6 anticipating d4 in response which would blunder mate in 1 Qh7#
However, he decides on principled chess and plays "the best move" Nf6. Interestingly the engine is fully in favor of hope chess suggesting that Bd6 was, in fact, the objectively best move. } 10. f4 { [%clk 0:04:39] } Qc7 { [%clk 0:03:55] with Bd6 and Bc6 ideas and possibly even O-O-O } 11. d4 { [%clk 0:04:32] } Bd6 { [%clk 0:03:50] } 12. Be3 { [%clk 0:04:28] } O-O { [%clk 0:03:46] Castling short after all. On f5, e5. (leaving the f pawn to block the LSB) } 13. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:23] } cxd4 { [%clk 0:03:43] } 14. cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:20] Bxd4 would have dropped the f pawn. (or Bc5 forcing the trade and then Qxc5+) } (14. Bxd4 Bc5 15. Bxc5 Qxc5+) 14... Rac8 { [%clk 0:03:40] rook to the open file with Qxc2 ideas. } 15. Rc1 { [%clk 0:04:18] } Qb6 { [%clk 0:03:37] Hit the b pawn but also Bb5 ideas. } 16. Nb3 { [%clk 0:04:16] with Nc5 ideas? } Ne4 { [%clk 0:03:13] On Bxe4, dxe4 we get the bishop pair. } 17. Bb1 { [%clk 0:04:08] } Rxc1 { [%clk 0:03:10] } 18. Qxc1 { [%clk 0:04:05] } Rc8 { [%clk 0:03:07] } 19. Qd1 { [%clk 0:04:02] } Bb5 { [%clk 0:02:54] with f5 planned as a follow up, locking in the knight on e4. } 20. Bd3 { [%clk 0:03:52] } Bxd3 { [%clk 0:02:51] happy to trade, up a pawn. } 21. Qxd3 { [%clk 0:03:50] } g6 { [%clk 0:02:37] Aman thinks about f5 "which also stops f5" but elects for g6 which does something similar while also giving his king an escape square (although on the same color as our opponent's DSB his pawn frozen on d4 gives us cover on that diagonal). f5 sill planned as a future move, especially if our opponent plays g4. } 22. Bc1 { [%clk 0:03:38] } Qc6 { [%clk 0:02:32] not attacking the bishop, but looking to play Qc2 and offer a queen trade. On Na5, Qxc1 works, as we actually win the queen in that line with a royal fork. Na5 Qxc1 24. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 25. Kh2 Rh1+ 26. Kxh1 Nf2+ 27. Kh2 Nxd3 } 23. Nc5 { [%clk 0:03:14] } (23. Na5 Qxc1 24. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 25. Kh2 Rh1+ 26. Kxh1 Nf2+ 27. Kh2 Nxd3) 23... Bxc5 { [%clk 0:02:23] Takes, rather reluctantly "I think we gotta take". But seems good as it wins a pawn? } 24. dxc5 { [%clk 0:03:12] } Qxc5+ { [%clk 0:02:23] } 25. Be3 { [%clk 0:03:09] } Qc7 { [%clk 0:01:49] Aman considered Qc4 or Qc2 offering a queen trade but decided that he wanted to bait the opponent into playing Rc1 (which would walk into the royal fork discussed in a variation a couple moves back). } 26. Bd4 { [%clk 0:03:06] } Qc2 { [%clk 0:01:35] } 27. Qb5 { [%clk 0:02:45] } Qc4 { [%clk 0:01:26] forces the queen exchange, as there is no way to avoid while saving the bishop. } 28. Qxc4 { [%clk 0:02:38] } Rxc4 { [%clk 0:01:25] } 29. Bxa7 { [%clk 0:02:34] } Ra4 { [%clk 0:01:23] forking bishop and a pawn. } 30. Bd4?? { [%clk 0:02:28] blundering the bishop. } Rxd4 { [%clk 0:01:22] } 31. b3 { [%clk 0:02:24] } b5 { [%clk 0:01:20] } 32. Rc1 { [%clk 0:02:21] } b4 { [%clk 0:01:18] } 33. Rc8+ { [%clk 0:02:18] spite check, of course. } Kg7 { [%clk 0:01:18] h5 ideas to follow } 34. g4 { [%clk 0:02:14] } Rd2 { [%clk 0:01:15] cutting the king off } 35. f5 { [%clk 0:02:11] } gxf5 { [%clk 0:01:14] } 36. gxf5 { [%clk 0:02:09] } exf5 { [%clk 0:01:14] } 37. Rc5 { [%clk 0:02:02] Here Aman discussed mating nets. Rook and knight have almost no mating nets unless the king is forced to the corner. Hence if we want to mate we need a king or pawn to participate, while the knight effectively stalemates the king, the king or pawn will support the rook to deliver mate. } f4 { [%clk 0:00:48] } 38. Rb5 { [%clk 0:01:59] } f3 { [%clk 0:00:42] setting up Rg2# once we have the knight on g3. } 39. Rxb4 { [%clk 0:01:52] } h5 { [%clk 0:00:34] Stopping Rg4+ } 40. h4 { [%clk 0:01:50] mate is now unavoidable. } Ng3 { [%clk 0:00:33] } 41. Rf4 { [%clk 0:01:43] } Rg2# { [%clk 0:00:31] 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

