{ https://youtu.be/LU87kQCjL1g?si=9qAaX1aYVCpdYWNn&t=1013 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:56] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:55] Winawer } Bb4 { [%clk 0:04:49] with the intention to take on c3 } 4. e5 { [%clk 0:04:54] } Ne7 { [%clk 0:04:43] we are not playing the main line c5 but a sideline favored by Aman. We will be looking to play b6 and Ba6 in order to trade off our bad French bishop ASAP. } 5. a3 { [%clk 0:04:52] } Bxc3+ { [%clk 0:04:42] } 6. bxc3 { [%clk 0:04:50] } b6 { [%clk 0:04:40] } 7. f4 { [%clk 0:04:49] } Ba6 { [%clk 0:04:37] } 8. Bd3 { [%clk 0:04:45] } Bxd3 { [%clk 0:04:35] we are happy with this trade. Our opponent may gain a tempo recapturing with development but we have saved a tempo by not needing to capture with our knight to the side of the board and then needing to maneuver back. } 9. cxd3 { [%clk 0:04:44] they recapture with a pawn anyway. Maybe looking for a solid central pawn structure denying us the e4 square. } Nbc6 { [%clk 0:04:30] we continue with normal development. This knight can head to c4 via a5. } 10. a4 { [%clk 0:04:39] } Qd7 { [%clk 0:04:28] The queen belongs on d7 in this set up. We are playing on the light squares. h5 and Nf5 are the sort of moves we should be looking at. } 11. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:37] } h5 { [%clk 0:04:27] we are controlling the g4 square with this move. We don't want our knight on f5 being booted. Prepares Nf5. } 12. Nh4 { [%clk 0:04:27] } g6 { [%clk 0:04:23] Aman doesn't explain this. A slight inaccuracy according to the engine. But kingside pawns often seem to end up in this configuration during this speed run. Perhaps giving us the option to recapture gxf5 should our opponent look to trade knights thus keeping our central pawns intact? NB: no that wasn't the reason, as Aman takes back exf5 later in the video. He did discuss the two options though, so perhaps flexibility here was one of the considerations in playing g6? } 13. O-O { [%clk 0:04:26] } Nf5 { [%clk 0:04:12] } 14. Nxf5 { [%clk 0:04:18] } exf5 { [%clk 0:03:56] Aman debated gxf5 and exf5 here. He had concerns about Ba3 in the exf5 line, which might make king safety and issue. The idea is to route the knight via d8 to e6 and create a blockade. Aman chose exf5 as the "spicier" option but did not explain what response he had to Ba3. } 15. Qb3 { [%clk 0:03:59] unfortunately our opponent does not play Ba3 so we'll never know what Aman had planned. Ba3 was the top engine move. } O-O { [%clk 0:03:46] Taking advantage of our opponent not playing Ba3 and getting our king safe. Rd8 planned as a follow up. } 16. Ba3 { [%clk 0:03:57] a little late but still the top engine move. Engine evaluates the position as dead equal. } Rfc8 { [%clk 0:03:42] Rc8 played instead of Rd8.
Aman added the explanation that "since c4 can't happen I am buying time for Nd8 Ne6" so maybe. Rc8 is better to leave the d8 square open for maneuvers and the rook on c8 defends a weak pawn? } 17. Bc1 { [%clk 0:03:45] our opponent seems to be maneuvering the bishop over to the king side, perhaps to f6? } Nd8 { [%clk 0:03:39] } 18. Be3 { [%clk 0:03:39] } Ne6 { [%clk 0:03:25] } 19. Rac1 { [%clk 0:03:27] Aman considered c5 as an idea here but I suppose played this first because the knight will be here in any line we play. } c5 { [%clk 0:03:23] and now the c5 idea. With c4 ideas. (in-line with the overarching plan to play on the light squares). } 20. dxc5 { [%clk 0:03:24] denying c4. } bxc5 { [%clk 0:03:17] again with c4 ideas. Rab8 also on the radar, to potentially remove the queen as a defender of the a4 pawn and win with our queen. } 21. Qb5 { [%clk 0:03:06] } Qc7 { [%clk 0:02:48] "Qxb5 is not good enough". Rab8 planned as a follow up. } 22. d4 { [%clk 0:02:57] } Rab8 { [%clk 0:02:34] On Qa6, Rb6 with ideas to play Rcb8 and double the rooks. On queen back, c4 and Qc6 ideas. } 23. Qd3 { [%clk 0:02:17] helps us as now c4 comes with temp. Qe2 was required. } c4 { [%clk 0:02:32] } 24. Qc2 { [%clk 0:02:14] } Rb3 { [%clk 0:02:30] } 25. Rb1 { [%clk 0:02:05] } Rcb8 { [%clk 0:02:29] On Rxb3, cxb3 } 26. Rxb3 { [%clk 0:02:01] } cxb3 { [%clk 0:02:23] passed pawn. } 27. Qb2 { [%clk 0:01:51] } Qc4 { [%clk 0:02:20] } 28. g3 { [%clk 0:01:41] appears to be offering us the a pawn. Perhaps a5 or Ra1 was stronger. } Qxa4 { [%clk 0:01:52] Ra1 but then we have Qd7 and that holds things together? } 29. Ra1 { [%clk 0:01:38] } Qd7 { [%clk 0:01:51] } 30. Ra3 { [%clk 0:01:15] } Qb7 { [%clk 0:01:50] } 31. Kf2 { [%clk 0:01:05] } a6 { [%clk 0:01:21] Aman is wondering what he should do with his knight. It has done a good job but now needs to move to play an active roll in the game. For the time being he plays the simple a6 with Qb5 ideas and slowly move the a pawn up the board to support the passed b pawn with will then free up our pieces for C8 Qc4. If he ever moves his queen, b2 is automatic. } 32. h4 { [%clk 0:01:05] } Qb5 { [%clk 0:01:17] } 33. Ke1 { [%clk 0:00:45] } a5 { [%clk 0:01:16] } 34. Kf2 { [%clk 0:00:38] } a4 { [%clk 0:01:15] } 35. Ke1 { [%clk 0:00:33] } Rc8 { [%clk 0:01:11] with Qd3 ideas, targeting the weak c pawn. } 36. Bd2 { [%clk 0:00:29] } Rc6 { [%clk 0:00:59] } 37. Kf2 { [%clk 0:00:27] Nxd4, cxd4, Rc2 winning here? } Nxd4 { [%clk 0:00:38] 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

