night) the opponent had Rf2 and Ne3.
Exchange sac Rxc2, Qxc2, Nxd4 tempting but not "technically working" after Qf2?
h5 is played with ideas to stop g4 booting our knight. } 14. a3?? { [%clk 0:03:42] } Qa4 { [%clk 0:01:30] Previously this did not work because of the b3 response but our opponent's a pawn push has squandered this resource. Putting pressure on the weak d pawn. (and a double attack on the knight and d pawn) . } 15. Ne3 { [%clk 0:03:38] best move in the position according to the engine but dropping the d pawn. The blunder was the a3 move played previously. } Qxd4 { [%clk 0:01:14] } 16. Kh1 { [%clk 0:03:30] } Qxd1 { [%clk 0:01:07] } 17. Nxd1 { [%clk 0:03:25] } Bc5 { [%clk 0:01:03] with ideas to dominate the knight/bishop and prevent Ne3 or Be3 and prevent development. } 18. Nc3 { [%clk 0:03:23] } h4 { [%clk 0:00:59] setting up check mate ideas. On h3, Ng3 forks king and rook. If he doesn't play h3, Ng3# (Taimanov's Knight Check, "How to Beat Your Dad at Chess", Chandler, Murry, Checkmate pattern #9) } 19. Rf3! { [%clk 0:03:12] good move } Ng3+ { [%clk 0:00:53] } 20. Rxg3 { [%clk 0:03:06] } hxg3 { [%clk 0:00:52] } 21. h3 { [%clk 0:03:05] } Kd7 { [%clk 0:00:49] Connecting the rooks. } 22. Na4 { [%clk 0:03:02] } Bd4 { [%clk 0:00:43] } 23. Bd2 { [%clk 0:02:57] } Rc2 { [%clk 0:00:40] } 24. Bc3 { [%clk 0:02:53] } Rxh3+!! { [%clk 0:00:39] } 25. gxh3 { [%clk 0:02:50] } Rh2# { [%clk 0:00:39] 0-1 Black wins. } 0-1

