tting down the bishops? He doesn't really explain beyond that he thinks the position being locked up is good for black in this case. } 16. Qc2 { [%clk 0:04:02] } b5 { [%clk 0:02:18] with b4 ideas. } 17. Nh4 { [%clk 0:03:58] Planning a Nxg6 sac to open up the kingside? } Rg8 { [%clk 0:02:16] Defending against the sac. } 18. Kh1 { [%clk 0:03:47] } Qb6 { [%clk 0:02:07] supporting the b4 push. } 19. Bxh5 { [%clk 0:03:42] this sac took Aman by surprise. } gxh5 { [%clk 0:02:03] } 20. Qh7 { [%clk 0:03:40] } Rf8 { [%clk 0:01:47] with ideas to run the king to the queenside. } 21. Qxh5 { [%clk 0:03:37] } Kd8 { [%clk 0:01:38] } 22. f5 { [%clk 0:03:21] our opponent is playing well, in an active way. } exf5 { [%clk 0:01:13] we don't want to allow f6. (trapping our bishop?) } 23. Nxf5 { [%clk 0:03:17] } Kc7 { [%clk 0:01:06] allowing Nxe7 as the lesser of the evils. } 24. Nd6 { [%clk 0:02:59] } Rh8 { [%clk 0:00:43] active defense } 25. Qxf7 { [%clk 0:02:42] our opponent now has 3 pawns for their piece sac. We want to get the queens off the board if we can. } Rcf8 { [%clk 0:00:39] } 26. Qg6 { [%clk 0:02:37] } Rfg8 { [%clk 0:00:35] On Qf8 we would probably play Rf8 once but probably not repeat after that. } 27. Qc2 { [%clk 0:02:15] } Nxe5 { [%clk 0:00:24] dxe5 drops the bishop to Qxe3
