{ https://youtu.be/LU87kQCjL1g?si=O58DYg5PPtwIKA8N&t=4313 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:05:00] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:59] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:59] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:58] } Bb4 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 4. e5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } Ne7 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 5. a3 { [%clk 0:04:53] } Bxc3+ { [%clk 0:04:48] } 6. bxc3 { [%clk 0:04:52] } b6 { [%clk 0:04:48] } 7. f4 { [%clk 0:04:51] } Ba6 { [%clk 0:04:46] All standard Winawer moves so far. Ne7 b6 Ba6 offering to trade off our bad French bishop ASAP } 8. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } Bxf1 { [%clk 0:04:43] } 9. Kxf1 { [%clk 0:04:50] Comes with the bonus of denying our opponent castling rights this time. } Nbc6 { [%clk 0:04:39] f5 c4 now target squares for our knights. We want to be playing on the light squares. We want our pawns on light squares. } 10. Kf2 { [%clk 0:04:49] } h5 { [%clk 0:04:33] Another thematic move in this variation--taking control of the g4 square to prevent g4. } 11. Rf1 { [%clk 0:04:47] } Na5 { [%clk 0:04:31] Headed to c4. } 12. Qd3 { [%clk 0:04:44] } Qd7 { [%clk 0:04:27] Our queen will go to c6 or preferably a4. } 13. Kg1 { [%clk 0:04:41] } Qa4 { [%clk 0:04:25] blockading the a pawn. Further controlling the c4 square. Hitting the c2 pawn. } 14. Bd2 { [%clk 0:04:35] } Nc4 { [%clk 0:04:18] Nf5 next and we have achieved our desired set up. } 15. Nh4 { [%clk 0:04:33] } g6 { [%clk 0:04:15] Again this pawn formation. We play g6 here to deny f5 and fix the f4 pawn as a weakness. } 16. Rfb1 { [%clk 0:04:16] } a5 { [%clk 0:04:09] Preventing Rb4 } 17. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:07] } Nf5 { [%clk 0:04:03] We have achieved our set up and now have O-O-O ideas. The rooks with semi open files look scary but all the infiltration points are covered so in actuality the rooks can't make use of the open lines.
Kd7 also an idea. } 18. Rb3 { [%clk 0:03:49] } O-O-O { [%clk 0:03:58] On Ng5, Rd7 to defend. the weak f pawn. King b7... a7 ideas. } 19. Be1 { [%clk 0:03:41] } Kb7 { [%clk 0:03:52] headed to a7 } 20. Nd2 { [%clk 0:03:39] } Nfe3 { [%clk 0:03:47] On Nxc4 we can recapture Nxc4 or even dxc4, as we'd be attacking the rook so Qxe3 would not be possible. } 21. Bf2 { [%clk 0:03:28] } Nxd2 { [%clk 0:03:43] no matter which knight our opponent captures, the other knight is headed to c4. } 22. Qxd2 { [%clk 0:03:21] } Nc4 { [%clk 0:03:41] with a tempo on the queen. We try to keep a knight on c4 even if we abandon the f5 post. } 23. Qd3 { [%clk 0:03:18] } Rc8 { [%clk 0:03:25] supporting a c5 break. opening up the c file to attack the weak double isolated pawns with the heavy pieces. } 24. Rbb1 { [%clk 0:03:12] } Ka7 { [%clk 0:03:16] Getting out of the pin. ("red dot") Nxa3 doesn't look right. Appears to win a pawn but loses a knight to Rb3. } (24... Nxa3 25. Rb3) 25. Be1 { [%clk 0:03:03] } Rb8 { [%clk 0:02:55] On c5, dxc5, Rxc5, Bf2, Rc7, Bd4 and the bishop sits in a commanding position in the center of the board. We probably don't want to allow that, hence a change of plans.
Rhc8... c5 would then allow us to take back with the b pawn after dxc5, bxc5 keeping the bishop out. } (25... c5 26. dxc5 Rxc5 27. Bf2 Rc7 28. Bd4) 26. Qd1 { [%clk 0:03:00] } Rhc8 { [%clk 0:02:23] Ne3 looks like it wins a pawn but after Qe2, Nxc2, Qd1 our knight is pinned to our queen. Sure we have b5 there but it looks messy and complicated so Aman decides against. } (26... Ne3 27. Qe2 Nxc2 28. Qd1 { pinning the knight to the queen. }) 27. Qc1 { [%clk 0:02:56] } Ka6 { [%clk 0:02:03] Aman does not explain this unfortunately. } 28. Rb4 { [%clk 0:02:49] } axb4 { [%clk 0:01:46] Aman goes into the line believing that 2 rooks for the queen and getting a passed pawn makes us the winners of this exchange. The engine backs up his evaluation.
He also notes that white's bishop being so bad is factored in here because it is effectively like white being down a piece, especially when contrasted with our mighty knight. } 29. axb4 { [%clk 0:02:46] } b5 { [%clk 0:01:37] } 30. Rxa4+ { [%clk 0:02:43] } bxa4 { [%clk 0:01:37] } 31. Qa1 { [%clk 0:02:38] } Kb5 { [%clk 0:01:31] With Ra8 ideas. Rooks belong behind passed pawns. } 32. Qa2 { [%clk 0:02:35] } a3 { [%clk 0:01:27] } 33. Bh4 { [%clk 0:02:29] } Re8 { [%clk 0:01:21] To prevent any Be7 ideas. } 34. Kf2 { [%clk 0:02:24] } Ra8 { [%clk 0:01:19] } 35. Ke2 { [%clk 0:02:19] } Kc6 { [%clk 0:01:05] Now we need to work out how to break through. White's queen is stuck watching the passed pawn. If the white queen ever moves, a2 is immediate. } 36. h3 { [%clk 0:02:11] } Kd7 { [%clk 0:01:02] } 37. Be1 { [%clk 0:02:01] } Rg8 { [%clk 0:00:55] } 38. h4 { [%clk 0:01:58] } Rgf8 { [%clk 0:00:53] } 39. Kf3 { [%clk 0:01:54] } f6 { [%clk 0:00:50] opening up the position. } 40. Ke2 { [%clk 0:01:36] } fxe5 { [%clk 0:00:48] } 41. fxe5 { [%clk 0:01:32] } Rf4 { [%clk 0:00:38] } 42. g3 { [%clk 0:01:27] } Re4+ { [%clk 0:00:35] } 43. Kd1 { [%clk 0:01:20] } Rf8 { [%clk 0:00:34] with Rf1 ideas, looking to win the bishop, or even checkmate ideas. According to the engine, it's already forced mate in 5. } 44. Qb3 { [%clk 0:01:11] } Rf1 { [%clk 0:00:31] } 45. Qa4+ { [%clk 0:01:09] } Ke7 { [%clk 0:00:30] 0-1 Black wins on time. Nice try but it's forced mate in 2 here. } 0-1

