{ https://youtu.be/rH0atZvwYpw?si=KNM1TnUG5JmkVYUa&t=2119 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:58] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:51] } 3. e5 { [%clk 0:04:57] } c5 { [%clk 0:04:50] } 4. c3 { [%clk 0:04:56] } Qb6 { [%clk 0:04:45] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:54] } Bd7 { [%clk 0:04:42] Aman switches the move order up here. Usually we play Nc6 putting pressure on the d pawn. We often play Bd7 too so may transpose to our regular line. Not sure why he mixes it up here. } 6. Be2 { [%clk 0:04:52] } Bb5 { [%clk 0:04:37] Ok he has mentioned this in an earlier video. He mentions that one reason he likes to play Qb6 before Nc6 is that it gives him the option to play Bd7... Bb5 as a way to trade off the "bad" French bishop early. So perhaps he is deciding to showcase that here on the assumption 2000 elo players are not going to blunder their queenside pawns/ central pawn chain. } 7. O-O { [%clk 0:04:41] } Bxe2 { [%clk 0:04:34] } 8. Qxe2 { [%clk 0:04:41] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:32] we now transpose with the opponent's queen a bit offside and no LSB discovered attack shenanigans to be worried about. 3 attackers to two defenders on that d4 pawn. } 9. Rd1 { [%clk 0:04:33] adding a defender to the d4 pawn. } cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:07] } 10. cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:33] } Nge7 { [%clk 0:04:05] With Nf5 ideas. } 11. Na3 { [%clk 0:04:31] } Nf5 { [%clk 0:03:59] } 12. Nc2 { [%clk 0:04:29] } h5 { [%clk 0:03:47] Stopping g4. I wonder why he doesn't play this before the Nf5 jump? Before he moved this he was looking at a DSB move so perhaps this h5 indirectly prepares Be7 in that without it g4 would boot the knight to the side of the board if there was no e7 square available to retreat to / re-route via. } 13. a3 { [%clk 0:04:27] a3 prompts Aman to think about moves like Na5 and Qb3. } Qb3 { [%clk 0:02:50] On Na5, b4 was a concern.
With Rc8 and Be7 ideas. } 14. Rd3 { [%clk 0:03:54] } Qb5 { [%clk 0:02:43] pinning the rook. } 15. b4 { [%clk 0:03:22] } Rc8 { [%clk 0:02:42] on a4, Qb6.
After that, on b5 we would play Na5. On a5 would would put the queen back Qb5 } 16. a4 { [%clk 0:02:58] } Qb6 { [%clk 0:02:40] } 17. b5 { [%clk 0:02:54] } Na5 { [%clk 0:02:39] With Nc4 ideas. } 18. Ne3 { [%clk 0:02:39] } Nxe3 { [%clk 0:02:25] Aman considered g6 but in the end elected to take. } 19. Bxe3 { [%clk 0:02:24] Re-capturing with the bishop seems natural. "If the obvious move isn't a bad move may as well [play it]" } Be7 { [%clk 0:02:13] } 20. Rc1 { [%clk 0:02:12] } Rxc1+ { [%clk 0:02:09] } 21. Bxc1 { [%clk 0:02:11] } a6 { [%clk 0:02:00] } 22. Rc3 { [%clk 0:02:06] threatening Rc8+ picking up our rook. } Nc4 { [%clk 0:01:52] Blocking Rc8. Kd7 was also an option to cover the infiltration squares.
