{ https://youtu.be/rH0atZvwYpw?si=SgaCe4tVXnsvZzWR&t=2725 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. e4 { [%clk 0:04:58] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:56] } 3. Nd2 { [%clk 0:04:56] } c5 { [%clk 0:04:39] main line again. Perhaps we'll get to see Aman's h6... a6 in a later video. } 4. exd5 { [%clk 0:04:54] } Qxd5 { [%clk 0:04:36] again taking back with the queen, which can't be harassed by white's knights. } 5. Ngf3 { [%clk 0:04:53] } cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:36] } 6. Bc4 { [%clk 0:04:48] } Qd7 { [%clk 0:04:33] dropping back to d7 again. } 7. Nb3 { [%clk 0:04:45] Aman thinks this may be an inaccuracy before castling. } Bb4+ { [%clk 0:04:04] Computer doesn't love this move } 8. Bd2 { [%clk 0:04:43] } Bxd2+ { [%clk 0:04:03] } 9. Qxd2 { [%clk 0:04:43] Aman thinks the trade is better for us. The computer disagrees. } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:02] } 10. Nbxd4 { [%clk 0:04:25] giving the pawn back } Nxd4? { [%clk 0:03:58] The computer gives this as a 2.2 advantage to white. Not sure why but the line is complicated and seems to have something to do with king safety.
On Nxd4 we will have to be wary of Bb5 winning our queen.
We will play a6 to prevent Bb5 because we want to play a6 anyway to prepare b5... Bb7. } 11. Qxd4 { [%clk 0:03:56] Computer wanted Nxd4. Now the position is back to equal. } (11. Nxd4 Qc7 12. Nb5 Qe5+ 13. Be2 Bd7 14. Nd6+ Kf8 15. Nxf7 Kxf7 16. Qxd7+ Ne7 17. Qxb7 Rhf8 18. Qa6 Qxb2 19. O-O Qb6 20. Qd3 Nf5) 11... Qxd4 { [%clk 0:03:47] } 12. Nxd4 { [%clk 0:03:55] } a6 { [%clk 0:03:37] Bd7 was also an idea. Aman plays a6 because "maybe it maintains a few more pieces on the board". } 13. O-O-O { [%clk 0:03:45] Here Aman makes an interesting comment about pawn majorities and end games. As a rule of thumb, queenside majorities are better than kingside majorities, and smaller majorities are better than larger majorities. By which is meant, 2 vs 1 is better than 3 vs 2, for example, because it is easier to make a passed pawn the smaller the number of pawns are. (it takes fewer moves to make a passed pawn). So by that logic , if all the pieces were traded, white is better here going into the end game. Hence Aman's earlier stated desire to keep pieces on the board if possible. } Nf6 { [%clk 0:03:27] } 14. a4 { [%clk 0:02:21] } Bd7 { [%clk 0:03:20] Hitting the a4 pawn.
On a5... Rc8 and a rook life to c5 to bother the pawn? } 15. Bb3 { [%clk 0:02:08] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:03:08] "an annoying move". Our opponent can't play f3, as we have a fork Nf2 forking the rooks. So our opponent is forced to move a rook to defend f2 here. } 16. Rhf1 { [%clk 0:01:44] } a5 { [%clk 0:03:04] fixing a4 as a weakness. Knight can drop back to c5 hitting both bishop and pawn. } 17. c3 { [%clk 0:01:35] } Nc5 { [%clk 0:03:01] } 18. Bc2 { [%clk 0:01:19] } Nxa4 { [%clk 0:02:46] Aman chooses to take with the knight. For some reason he likes a B vs N end game here. Maybe because there are pawns on both sides of the board and the position is quite open. } 19. f4 { [%clk 0:01:16] Declining to simplify. } Nc5 { [%clk 0:02:20] On f5 we have e5 hitting the knight and maintaining cover on the e file to shield the king. } 20. Rde1 { [%clk 0:01:07] "f5 is coming. Not the end of the world, but it does need a reaction". } O-O { [%clk 0:01:32] } 21. f5 { [%clk 0:00:59] } exf5 { [%clk 0:01:30] } 22. Nxf5 { [%clk 0:00:59] has to be Nxf5 or else on Bxf5, Bxf5, Nd3 forking the rook and king. } Bxf5 { [%clk 0:01:22] } 23. Rxf5 { [%clk 0:00:57] } b6 { [%clk 0:01:20] } 24. Rh5 { [%clk 0:00:48] } g6 { [%clk 0:01:19] escape square and a free tempo on the rook. Note g6 rather than h6 as we want our escape square to be the opposite color of our opponent's bishop. } 25. Rh4 { [%clk 0:00:42] } Rfe8 { [%clk 0:01:16] } 26. Rf1 { [%clk 0:00:38] Now it's our opponent who wants to keep pieces on the board. Which [pawn] majority is best? The one that actually is a majority! } Re2 { [%clk 0:01:07] with Ra7 ideas (to defend the f7 pawn on Rhf4) } 27. Rhf4 { [%clk 0:00:34] } Ra7 { [%clk 0:01:05] defending the f pawn. } 28. b4 { [%clk 0:00:31] } axb4 { [%clk 0:01:03] } 29. cxb4 { [%clk 0:00:31] } Nb3+ { [%clk 0:00:35] Knight can't be taken. (or Ra1#)
In the post match analysis Aman admitted he thought this was better than it actually was and that Ra2 was better } 30. Kb2 { [%clk 0:00:25] } (30. Bxb3 Ra1#) 30... Nd2? { [%clk 0:00:29] On Rf2, Nc4+
