Here Aman notes that it is tough for our opponent to play f4 because the rook is not on f1, the bishop is not hitting f4. } 11. h3 { [%clk 0:04:34] } h6 { [%clk 0:03:05] With g5 ideas. Aman explains that white's only hope in this position is getting the f4 pawn break in. Our opponent is not getting b4, and we are going to get a6... b5. If we can prevent f4 then our opponent's position is almost hopeless. } 12. Nf1 { [%clk 0:04:25] } g5 { [%clk 0:03:01] With f6 planned to solidify. And Ng6 ideas. } 13. N1h2 { [%clk 0:04:17] } f6 { [%clk 0:02:51] If Ng4, Kf7. } 14. g4 { [%clk 0:04:03] with ideas of Nf5 perhaps? } Kg7 { [%clk 0:02:34] Gives our rook access to the h file. } 15. h4 { [%clk 0:04:01] } Ng6 { [%clk 0:02:27] if hxg5 we are happy as the h file opens up. If he plays h5 we will play Nf4. } 16. h5 { [%clk 0:03:41] } Nf4 { [%clk 0:02:25] } 17. Bxf4 { [%clk 0:03:36] } exf4 { [%clk 0:02:14] Taking away from the center. Aman doesn't explain why. The engine clearly prefers it. Perhaps it's about keeping the king side locked up so that we can switch to playing on the queenside. Also we have a square for our knight Ne5. Also the pawn on f4 is nice in restricting the knight maneuvers. Nf1 and then what? } 18. Bh3 { [%clk 0:03:34] } Be6 { [%clk 0:02:02] Rb8... a6... b7 ideas } 19. a3 { [%clk 0:03:31] a3 but how can he play b4? } Rab8 { [%clk 0:01:58] } 20. Qc2 { [%clk 0:03:11] } a6 { [%clk 0:01:25] We don't play Ne5 yet because our opponent likely wants to trade to relieve his cramped position and improve his poor pieces. We should think of the trade not as our c6 knight for his f3 knight, but rather our c3 knight for his terrible h2 knight. Once you think of it like that it makes no sense whatsoever to facilitate a trade.
Aman considered playing b5 right away but elects for the preparatory move first. The engine likes b5 right away. } 21. Nd2 { [%clk 0:03:07] } Rfc8 { [%clk 0:01:21] } 22. f3 { [%clk 0:03:04] this is great to see. Now we can play Ne5 and there is no way our opponent can trade. } b5 { [%clk 0:01:12] } 23. b3 { [%clk 0:03:02] } Ne5 { [%clk 0:01:04] } 24. Kf2 { [%clk 0:02:55] } Qd8 { [%clk 0:01:01] Preparing to re-route the bishop Bc7 ... Ba5 ... Bc3 } 25. Reb1 { [%clk 0:02:51] } Bc7 { [%clk 0:00:57] } 26. Ra2 { [%clk 0:02:24] } Ba5 { [%clk 0:00:55] } 27. b4? { [%clk 0:02:14] the position opening up favors us. c4 pawn is pinned to the rook. } Bb6 { [%clk 0:00:46] } 28. Bf1 { [%clk 0:01:56] } cxb4 { [%clk 0:00:35] intending bxc4 next and opening up the diagonal towards the opponent's king. } 29. axb4 { [%clk 0:01:52] } bxc4 { [%clk 0:00:33] } 30. Rxa6 { [%clk 0:01:35] } cxd3 { [%clk 0:00:32] } 31. Qa4 { [%clk 0:01:08] } Rc2 { [%clk 0:00:29] } 32. Ke1 { [%clk 0:01:05] } Qc7 { [%clk 0:00:22] Rxd2 a threat. Qc3 a threat. Rc1 also an idea } 33. Kd1 { [%clk 0:00:49] } Qc3 { [%clk 0:00:16] 0-1 Black wins. Ra2 an idea. } 0-1

