{ https://youtu.be/rH0atZvwYpw?si=zlzY17bhLG9Uba-9&t=5005 } 1. e4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:57] } 2. d4 { [%clk 0:04:53] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:55] } 3. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } Bb4 { [%clk 0:04:52] } 4. Ne2 { [%clk 0:04:46] first time we've seen this move. According to this lecture https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HtzVDvVYRaU this is the most common side line (with the e5 advanced being the mainline)
Aman explains two things here: 1) In this line, he does not like to play Bxc3 on a3, because the knight can just be replaced with another knight. i.e. we are not damaging our opponent's pawns as compensation. And Aman has a thing in general about not liking to take a knight with a bishop when it can just be replaced with the second knight. 2) We don't want to take the gambitted pawn. In Aman's experience the opponent wins the pawn back quite easily and the resulting position is not as easy to play. } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:30] We don't want to play Bxc3 because it just gets replaced by a knight. We don't want to take dxe4 even though it looks like a free pawn. (although the engine likes dxe4) because eventually white is able to get it back. Aman doesn't address blocking the c pawn with his knight. Perhaps the opponent's c pawn also being blocked by their knight (which is pinned) is a factor. } 5. e5 { [%clk 0:04:34] } f6 { [%clk 0:04:27] "when the knight is not on f3 we need to assault the center." engine wants a3 or f4 in response to this.
Aman also explained "we are going to have much less space than our opponent, but we might be able to open things up quickly. That's the idea". } 6. exf6?! { [%clk 0:04:18] 3rd best engine move but the evaluation is slightly worse for white. } Qxf6 { [%clk 0:04:12] Nxf6 was also fine, but we are taking with the queen, with the idea of Ne7... Nf5 hitting the d pawn.
Also, the queen will be well placed to attack f2 after we castle. } 7. Be3 { [%clk 0:04:08] } Nge7 { [%clk 0:04:09] } 8. a3 { [%clk 0:03:47] } Ba5 { [%clk 0:04:06] we can retreat to b6 if required. (from where it will put pressure on the d4 pawn) O-O also planned. } 9. Nf4 { [%clk 0:02:59] "I would chalk that move up to ambitious". By that Aman means that although he get the opponent is trying to get his bishop out, he doesn't think that this is the moment to play so aggressively. We have a lot of attacking potential after Nf5, but our opponent does not seem to have the defensive resources needed to cope. } Nf5 { [%clk 0:03:38] Bd7... O-O (or maybe O-O-O depending on what our opponent does, but we prefer O-O) planned } 10. Nh5 { [%clk 0:02:43] } Qf7 { [%clk 0:03:31] eyeballing the knight. 1 square diagonal away so the knight can't bother us again any time soon. } 11. Be2 { [%clk 0:02:08] } O-O { [%clk 0:03:07] } 12. O-O { [%clk 0:01:53] } e5 { [%clk 0:02:32] with d4 ideas.
Aman plays this knowing it takes the game in a tactical direction. It looks good for us but it complicates the position and might make things dicy. He explains that one factor in his thinking here is that he is up on the clock vs our opponent. The opponent is taking a lot of time to think so adding tactical complications is further pressuring them to burn up their clock time. } 13. Nxd5 { [%clk 0:00:38] The expected response } Qxd5 { [%clk 0:02:16] Opponent may have c4 b4 ideas to try and trap the bishop. } 14. c4 { [%clk 0:00:34] } Qf7 { [%clk 0:01:55] } 15. d5 { [%clk 0:00:33] Not expected. b4 would have won back the piece but the line I put as a variation here has us with better pawn structure and winning at the end. This d5 move is the top engine move. It's still evaluated as winning for black but it's slightly better for white than the b4 line. } (15. b4 Nfxd4 16. bxa5 Be6 17. Bxd4 Rad8 18. Ng3 exd4 { passed pawn, and opponent's a pawns doubled. Engine gives this -1.8 in our favor. }) 15... Ncd4 { [%clk 0:01:36] } 16. b4 { [%clk 0:00:32] } Bb6 { [%clk 0:00:55] } 17. c5 { [%clk 0:00:30] } Qxd5 { [%clk 0:00:54] } 18. cxb6 { [%clk 0:00:27] } axb6 { [%clk 0:00:51] Opening up our rook. The evaluation here is -1.0 in our favor. We don't have the passed pawn like in the 15. b4 line and it's *our* pawns which are doubled but we are a pawn up.
With our queen on the g8-a2 diagonal lined up with the king we need to be wary of Bc4 ideas. } 19. Ng3 { [%clk 0:00:24] } Be6 { [%clk 0:00:34] } 20. Nxf5 { [%clk 0:00:21] } Nxf5 { [%clk 0:00:31] } 21. Bd4?? { [%clk 0:00:21] drops the bishop. not a pre-move but played under time pressure in 1 second and perhaps anticipating Bxf5 on the previous move? } Nxd4 { [%clk 0:00:25] } 22. Bd3 { [%clk 0:00:15] } e4 { [%clk 0:00:21] } 23. Bc2 { [%clk 0:00:14] } Rad8 { [%clk 0:00:19] } 24. Qe1 { [%clk 0:00:09] } Bh3?! { [%clk 0:00:10] Really not sure why Aman didn't play Nxc2 here. } 25. Qc3 { [%clk 0:00:09] } Ne2+! { [%clk 0:00:08] OK, he was anticipating this pre-move maybe. The dark arts are employed against another hapless victim suffocating under time pressure. } 26. Kh1 { [%clk 0:00:07] } Nxc3 { [%clk 0:00:08] } 27. Rad1 { [%clk 0:00:05] } Qg5 { [%clk 0:00:06] ruthless } 28. Rxd8 { [%clk 0:00:05] } Bxg2+ { [%clk 0:00:04] when you have mate in one, look for better. } 29. Kg1 { [%clk 0:00:03] } Ne2#! { [%clk 0:00:03] 0-1 Black wins. and a semi-suffocated mate for the crowd. } 0-1

