{ https://youtu.be/rH0atZvwYpw?si=7C0iw3Dovuuu6xvA&t=6251 } 1. d4 { [%clk 0:05:00] } e6 { [%clk 0:04:58] } 2. e4 { [%clk 0:04:57] } d5 { [%clk 0:04:51] transposes into the French } 3. e5 { [%clk 0:04:53] } c5 { [%clk 0:04:49] } 4. c3 { [%clk 0:04:51] } Qb6 { [%clk 0:04:44] } 5. Nf3 { [%clk 0:04:47] } Nc6 { [%clk 0:04:36] Here, for some reason, Aman goes back to the plan of pressuring the d pawn rather than the Bd7... Bb5 maneuver to trade off the bad French bishop. } 6. a3 { [%clk 0:04:46] going into a new variation for this speed run. White's idea appears to be b4 (allowing the bishop to be brought out without dropping the b pawn?)
In the post game analysis Aman said that a3 is a good response in the French advanced. This opponent played the advanced variation better than anyone we have played so far. } Bd7 { [%clk 0:04:02] standard developing move. } 7. b4 { [%clk 0:04:44] } cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:00] } 8. cxd4 { [%clk 0:04:43] } Nh6 { [%clk 0:03:54] offering the opponent a trade. Aman appears comfortable to open up the g file here. Nf5 if there is no trade. } 9. Nc3 { [%clk 0:04:36] } Nf5 { [%clk 0:03:42] } 10. Na4 { [%clk 0:04:35] } Qd8 { [%clk 0:03:36] } 11. Bb2 { [%clk 0:04:34] white planning Bd3 next? And the knight on a4 Nc5? } f6?! { [%clk 0:03:07] challenging the center. The position demands this according to Aman. Perhaps because we have less space? Engine doesn't like it. } (11... h5) 12. g4 { [%clk 0:04:21] engine wanted Bd3.
Interesting Aman didn't play or discuss the h5 idea to stop this g4 booting of the knight. The engine seemed to prefer that to the f6 move. } Nh6 { [%clk 0:02:41] Aman considered Ne7... Ng6 and had concerns about Nh6, exf6, Qxf6, g5 forking queen and knight. But he likes that Nh6 attacks the g4 pawn and exf6 can be met with gxf6. } 13. h3 { [%clk 0:03:57] in the post game analysis Aman highlighted this h3 move as a mistake and a turning point. The engine does back that up and the evaluation goes from slightly better for white to slightly better for black after this move and black's next. } Nf7 { [%clk 0:02:24] putting pressure on e5. We now have 3 attackers on it, vs 3 defenders (the bishop on b2 is indirectly defending)
On b5, we would put our knight on a5. Na5.
On Nc5, we would capture Bxc5 which would also help us castle. } 14. Bd3 { [%clk 0:03:29] "that move makes the most sense to me" says Aman. It is the second choice of the engine. } fxe5 { [%clk 0:02:11] "I think it might be time to take here, at least once". says Aman but not sure what he means by "at least once". We can't capture the e pawn as it is defended twice. } 15. dxe5 { [%clk 0:03:27] } g6 { [%clk 0:02:01] } (15... Ncxe5 16. Nxe5 Nxe5 17. Bxe5) 16. O-O { [%clk 0:02:58] } Bg7 { [%clk 0:02:00] Maybe the "at least once" meant that we are exchanging for now and then will look to add pressure and win the pawn later. Although Aman acknowledges that our opponent will always be able to defend it with moves like Qe2 or Re1 etc.
Aman explains that sometimes you want to attack something not necessary to win the pawn or piece, but because you want to tie down your opponent's pieces to defensive duties.
We need to be thinking of an answer to Nc5 which is surely coming. } 17. Re1 { [%clk 0:02:32] } b6 { [%clk 0:01:53] stopping Nc5.
On b5, Na5 or Ne7.
O-O probably will be the next move. } 18. b5 { [%clk 0:02:00] } Na5 { [%clk 0:01:38] Na5 is preferred over Ne7 because there does not appear to be any future for the knight on e7. (no where it could go next)
From here we have Nb7... Nc5 ideas. } 19. Nc3 { [%clk 0:01:51] Nxd5 a threat? } O-O { [%clk 0:01:28] Aman elects for king safety, concerned about Nxd5 ideas. He doesn't think it works because it hangs the bishop, but he is being wary. } 20. Qe2 { [%clk 0:01:41] now Nxd5 might start to work. } (20. Nxd5 exd5 21. e6 { forking our pieces but the bishop hangs with an attack on the rook. } Bxb2) 20... Re8 { [%clk 0:01:01] To prevent Nxd5.
A necessary evil, as our rook was well placed on the f file.
We want to get our knight to c4 as a follow up idea. Rc8 also planned. } 21. Na2 { [%clk 0:01:27] Knight headed to Nb4... Nc6 maybe? } Rc8 { [%clk 0:00:51] } 22. Nb4 { [%clk 0:01:23] } Nc4 { [%clk 0:00:49] Threatens the c pawn as the bishop's defense of the pawn is now blocked.
Here Aman pre-moves recapture with the rook. } 23. Bd4? { [%clk 0:01:11] drops the pawn. } Bxb5 { [%clk 0:00:45] } 24. a4 { [%clk 0:01:09] } Bd7 { [%clk 0:00:43] } 25. Rac1? { [%clk 0:01:02] dropping the a pawn. } Bxa4 { [%clk 0:00:37] We are taking the pawns offered. Ng5 is planned. } 26. Ra1 { [%clk 0:00:41] } Qd7 { [%clk 0:00:31] active defense. Also defends the a pawn, which is key as these outside connected passed pawns are a valuable asset for us in the position. On Qa2, b5. } 27. h4 { [%clk 0:00:31] } a5 { [%clk 0:00:28] driving the knight back. } 28. Nc2 { [%clk 0:00:24] } Bxc2 { [%clk 0:00:19] } 29. Bxc2 { [%clk 0:00:22] From here time is low for both players (sub 20s) so we enter something of a "flag situation".
Aman gives a master class in pre moving. He gives the advice later in the video that "if you can check your opponent I highly recommend it in any pre-move situation". } Qe7 { [%clk 0:00:13] } 30. h5 { [%clk 0:00:20] } gxh5 { [%clk 0:00:11] } 31. gxh5 { [%clk 0:00:20] } Ng5 { [%clk 0:00:10] } 32. Kf1 { [%clk 0:00:17] } Rf8 { [%clk 0:00:09] } 33. Nxg5 { [%clk 0:00:15] } Qxg5 { [%clk 0:00:09] } 34. Qd3 { [%clk 0:00:13] } Nd2+ { [%clk 0:00:05] } 35. Ke2 { [%clk 0:00:11] } Rxc2 { [%clk 0:00:05] } 36. Qxc2 { [%clk 0:00:10] } Ne4 { [%clk 0:00:04] } 37. Kd1 { [%clk 0:00:08] } Qxh5+ { [%clk 0:00:03] } 38. Kc1 { [%clk 0:00:06] } Qf5 { [%clk 0:00:03] } 39. Kb2 { [%clk 0:00:05] } Qf7 { [%clk 0:00:03] } 40. Ra4 { [%clk 0:00:03] } h6 { [%clk 0:00:03] } 41. Ra3 { [%clk 0:00:02] } b5 { [%clk 0:00:03] } 42. Rg3 { [%clk 0:00:01] } a4 { [%clk 0:00:03] } 43. Rxe4 { [%clk 0:00:00] } a3+ { [%clk 0:00:03] 0-1 Black wins on time. } 0-1

